---
layout: base.njk
pageTitle: "My Town News — Local News for Your Town, Every Friday"
pageDesc: "Free, independent local news for towns and neighborhoods across the country — a new local paper where the old one stopped. Published every Friday."
---

{# The national homepage.

   Until 2026-09-12 this was San Francisco's front page with a national band
   bolted on top: the hero said "San Francisco", the nav said "All San
   Francisco", and below the map sat an SF-only Leaflet map and a list of
   fourteen SF editions. Launching a second city would have landed a reader in
   Waxahachie on a page about the Marina.

   San Francisco's map and edition list now live at /san-francisco/, which is
   to that city what this page is to the country. Every city's hub gets the
   same treatment as it grows past one edition. #}
{% set liveCities = cities | selectattr("live") | list %}

{# ── Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<header class="masthead home-hero" role="banner">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="home-hero__eyebrow">Free &amp; Independent · Every Friday</p>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">My Town <em>News</em></h1>
    <p class="home-hero__desc">
      {% if liveCities | length > 1 %}
      Local news for {{ liveCities | length }} towns and cities — written for the places whose own paper closed, and delivered to your inbox every Friday.
      {% else %}
      Local news written for the places whose own paper closed — delivered to your inbox every Friday.
      {% endif %}
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="gold-rule"></div>
</header>

{# ── Sticky nav ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
<nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Site navigation">
  <div class="site-nav__inner container">
    <a class="site-nav__logo" href="/" aria-label="My Town News home">{% inlineMark %}</a>
    <span class="site-nav__label">My Town News</span>
    <ul class="site-nav__links" role="list">
      <li><a href="#editions">Editions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#this-week">This Week</a></li>
      <li><a href="#newsletter">Newsletter</a></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="site-nav__right">
      <a class="site-nav__aux" href="/about/">About</a>
      <a class="site-nav__subscribe-btn" href="/subscribe/">Subscribe</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>

<main id="main">

  {# ── National map ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Where My Town News publishes, nationally. Live cities only: the footer
     rule that unlaunched editions must not be advertised applies here too,
     and a map is a worse place to break it than a footer, because a dot
     reads as a promise.

     It appears only once there are at least two live cities. A map of the
     United States with a single dot on it argues against the thing it is
     meant to argue for, and hiding it until it earns its place means nobody
     has to remember to switch it on — it switches itself on with the second
     launch.

     No Leaflet, no tile server, no API key. The outline is one cached SVG
     generated by scripts/build-us-outline.js and the dots are absolutely
     positioned at percentages computed at build time by lib/us-projection.js.
     After CARTO started watermarking our tiles in August and a missing
     leaflet.css turned the neighborhood map into a column of images in
     September, a map with no third-party runtime dependency at all is worth
     more than an interactive one. #}
  <section id="editions" class="us-map-section" aria-labelledby="us-map-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="us-map-section__intro">
        <h2 class="us-map-section__title" id="us-map-heading">Find Your Edition</h2>
        <p class="us-map-section__sub">
          {% if liveCities | length > 1 %}
          {{ liveCities | length }} towns and cities. A new local paper where the old one stopped.
          {% else %}
          Enter your ZIP code and we'll take you to your edition.
          {% endif %}
        </p>
      </div>

      {# ── ZIP lookup, above the map ──────────────────────────────────────
         Ahead of the map because typing five digits beats scanning a map of
         the United States for your own town, and because it is the control
         that answers the question a first-time visitor actually has: is
         there one for me?

         It indexes every live edition in every city, not San Francisco's.
         Moving an SF-only lookup to the top of a national page would be
         worse than leaving it where it was — a reader in Staunton would be
         told they are outside the coverage area of a paper that covers
         them. #}
      <div class="zip-lookup zip-lookup--national">
        <label class="zip-lookup__label" for="zip-input">Find your edition by ZIP code</label>
        <div class="zip-lookup__row">
          <input class="zip-lookup__input" id="zip-input" type="text"
                 inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]{5}"
                 maxlength="5" placeholder="e.g. 94133" aria-label="Enter your ZIP code">
          <button class="zip-lookup__btn" id="zip-btn" type="button">Go</button>
        </div>
        <div class="zip-lookup__result" id="zip-result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

        {# Revealed by the lookup when a ZIP is not covered. Hidden rather than
           built in JavaScript so that the form is real markup a browser can
           post without us assembling it by hand, and so the metadata field
           below is visible in the page source rather than buried in a string.

           A native POST to Buttondown's embed endpoint, exactly as the
           subscribe page and every edition page already do it. No API key
           reaches the browser — that key can mail the entire list. #}
        <form class="zip-waitlist embeddable-buttondown-form" id="zip-waitlist" hidden
              action="https://buttondown.com/api/emails/embed-subscribe/{{ site.buttondownUser }}"
              method="post">
          <input type="hidden" name="embed" value="1">
          <input type="hidden" name="tag" value="waitlist">
          {# Underscores, not hyphens: Buttondown's template language cannot
             read {{ subscriber.metadata.requested-zip }}. #}
          <input type="hidden" name="metadata__requested_zip" id="zip-waitlist-zip" value="">
          <label class="zip-waitlist__label" for="zip-waitlist-email">
            Leave your address and we'll tell you if we get there.
          </label>
          <div class="zip-lookup__row">
            <input class="zip-lookup__input" id="zip-waitlist-email" name="email" type="email"
                   placeholder="you@example.com" required autocomplete="email">
            <button class="zip-lookup__btn" type="submit">Notify me</button>
          </div>
        </form>
      </div>

      {% if liveCities | length > 1 %}
      {# Decorative on its own: the list below is the navigation, and it is the
         only version that exists on a phone. A US map at 340px with five dots
         on it is a picture, not a control. #}
      <div class="us-map" aria-hidden="true">
        <img class="us-map__outline" src="/assets/img/us-outline.svg" alt="" width="1025" height="604" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        {% for city in liveCities %}
        {% set pt = city | usPoint %}
        {% if pt %}
        <a class="us-map__pin" href="{{ city.slug | cityPath }}" tabindex="-1"
           style="left:{{ pt.left | round(2) }}%;top:{{ pt.top | round(2) }}%">
          <span class="us-map__dot"></span>
          <span class="us-map__label">{{ city.name }}<span class="us-map__state">{{ city.state }}</span></span>
        </a>
        {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </div>

      <ul class="us-map-list" role="list">
        {% for city in liveCities %}
        <li>
          <a href="{{ city.slug | cityPath }}">
            <span class="us-map-list__name">{{ city.name }}<span class="us-map-list__state">{{ city.state }}</span></span>
            <span class="us-map-list__tag">{{ city.tagline }}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </section>

  {# ── This Week — Editor's Picks ──────────────────────────────────────── #}
  <section id="this-week" class="editors-picks" aria-labelledby="picks-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-head__label" id="picks-heading">This Week — Editor's Picks</h2>
        <span class="section-head__line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>

      {# One card per live city: the lead story from whichever of that city's
         editions published most recently.

         This was a single hardcoded North Waterfront story with an "All SF
         Neighborhoods" grid beneath it — both written when San Francisco was
         the only city, and the grid pointed exclusively at SF editions.

         A city with no issue yet is shown without a story rather than hidden.
         Every city spends its first week in exactly that state, and a homepage
         that quietly omits a town we have announced is worse than one that
         says the first issue is coming. #}
      <div class="city-picks">
        {% for city in liveCities %}
        {% set lead = city.slug | cityLead(clusters) %}
        <article class="city-pick">
          <div class="city-pick__head">
            <h3 class="city-pick__city">
              <a href="{{ city.slug | cityPath }}">{{ city.name }}<span class="city-pick__state">{{ city.state }}</span></a>
            </h3>
            {% if lead %}<span class="city-pick__week">{{ lead.issue.weekOf | date_short }}</span>{% endif %}
          </div>

          {% if lead %}
          <span class="nbadge" style="--accent:{{ lead.edition.accent }};border-color:{{ lead.edition.accent }};color:{{ lead.edition.accent }}">{{ lead.edition.name }}</span>
          <h4 class="city-pick__headline">{{ lead.story.headline }}</h4>
          {% if lead.story.dek %}<p class="city-pick__dek">{{ lead.story.dek }}</p>{% endif %}
          <p class="city-pick__body">{{ (lead.story.body or "") | excerpt(45) }}</p>
          <a class="city-pick__link" style="color:{{ lead.edition.accent }};border-color:{{ lead.edition.accent }}"
             href="{{ lead.edition | editionPath(clusters) }}">Read {{ lead.edition.name }} &rarr;</a>
          {% else %}
          <p class="city-pick__body">{{ city.tagline }}</p>
          <p class="city-pick__soon">First issue coming Friday.</p>
          {% endif %}
        </article>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  {# ── Newsletter ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
  <section id="newsletter" aria-labelledby="newsletter-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="newsletter">
        <p class="newsletter__eyebrow">Free. Independent. Weekly.</p>
        <h2 class="newsletter__headline" id="newsletter-heading">Get My Town News<br>every Friday morning</h2>
        <p class="newsletter__sub">
          Local news, events, and your community directory — every edition, every week.
        </p>
        {# This was a form with no action attribute and no JavaScript behind it.
           Typing an address and pressing Subscribe reloaded the page with a
           query string and subscribed nobody — on the most-visited page on the
           site, silently, for as long as it has existed. Every edition page has
           posted to Buttondown correctly the whole time.

           It cannot simply be pointed at Buttondown: a national page has no one
           tag to apply, and an untagged subscribe mails the person every edition
           in the country. So it goes to /subscribe/, where they choose — which
           is also the page that now groups editions by city. A button that works
           beats a form that does not. #}
        <a class="newsletter__cta" href="/subscribe/">Choose your editions &rarr;</a>
        <p class="newsletter__note">Free. No spam. Unsubscribe from any edition anytime.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

{% include "partials/footer.njk" %}

<script>
(function () {
  /* ZIP → edition index, built at build time from clusters.json.
     Every live edition in every city, not just San Francisco's: the lookup
     now sits at the top of a national page, and a Staunton reader being told
     they are outside the coverage area would be a bug with a straight face.

     A ZIP spanning two editions produces an array with both, so the reader is
     offered a choice rather than silently sent to one of them. That happens
     within a city (94111 is Downtown and North Waterfront) and could one day
     happen across cities, which is why the city name is carried through. */
  var zipIndex = {};
  [
    {% for c in clusters %}{% if c.live %}{% for z in c.zipCodes %}
    { zip: "{{ z }}", name: {{ c.name | dump | safe }}, city: {{ c.city | dump | safe }}, url: {{ (c | editionPath(clusters)) | dump | safe }} },
    {% endfor %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
  ].forEach(function (e) {
    if (!zipIndex[e.zip]) zipIndex[e.zip] = [];
    zipIndex[e.zip].push({ name: e.name, city: e.city, url: e.url });
  });

  var input    = document.getElementById('zip-input');
  var btn      = document.getElementById('zip-btn');
  var result   = document.getElementById('zip-result');
  var waitlist = document.getElementById('zip-waitlist');
  var wlZip    = document.getElementById('zip-waitlist-zip');
  if (!input || !btn || !result) return;

  function escapeHtml(s) {
    return String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, function (ch) {
      return { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[ch];
    });
  }

  function hideWaitlist() {
    if (waitlist) waitlist.hidden = true;
  }

  function lookup() {
    var zip = (input.value || '').trim();

    /* Reset */
    result.innerHTML = '';
    result.className = 'zip-lookup__result';
    hideWaitlist();

    if (!/^\d{5}$/.test(zip)) {
      result.textContent = 'Please enter a 5-digit ZIP code.';
      result.className += ' zip-lookup__result--warn';
      return;
    }

    var matches = zipIndex[zip];

    if (!matches || matches.length === 0) {
      /* Most visitors will land here for a long time, so this message is the
         one that matters most on the page. It says what is true, asks for the
         thing that actually helps — someone telling us where to go next — and
         offers to remember them. It does not imply a town is coming. */
      result.innerHTML =
        'We don’t publish in ' + escapeHtml(zip) + ' yet. ' +
        'If you’d like your community added, email us at ' +
        '<a href="mailto:info@mytown.news?subject=' + encodeURIComponent('Please cover ' + zip) + '">info@mytown.news</a>.';
      result.className += ' zip-lookup__result--warn';
      if (waitlist && wlZip) {
        wlZip.value = zip;
        waitlist.hidden = false;
      }
      return;
    }

    if (matches.length === 1) {
      window.location.href = matches[0].url;
      return;
    }

    /* ZIP spans more than one edition — let the reader choose. Labelled with
       the city, because "Waxahachie" and "North Waterfront" side by side
       without one is a guess. */
    result.innerHTML = 'ZIP ' + escapeHtml(zip) + ' is covered by ' + matches.length + ' editions: ' +
      matches.map(function (m) {
        return '<a href="' + m.url + '">' + escapeHtml(m.name) +
               (m.city && m.city !== m.name ? ' <span class="zip-lookup__city">' + escapeHtml(m.city) + '</span>' : '') +
               '</a>';
      }).join(' &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; ');
    result.className += ' zip-lookup__result--multi';
  }

  btn.addEventListener('click', lookup);
  input.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') lookup();
  });
})();
</script>
